  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "pQMCfri9e0sPzFLkk19jLweoSTc=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-13T14:34:30.231Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "`ProcessorFilter` looks uniform, which is the source of the bug:\n\n```typescript\ntype ProcessorFilter = {\n  documentType?: string[];\n  scope?: string[];\n  branch?: string[];\n  documentId?: string[];\n};\n```\n\nMatching is per field, and all present fields must match. A field that is `undefined` or `[]` matches everything; a field with values matches an operation whose corresponding context value is in the array.\n\nThe `\"*\"` wildcard is special-cased in **`documentId` only**. There it means \"any document\". In `documentType`, `scope`, and `branch` there is no wildcard handling at all \u2014 a literal `\"*\"` is compared as a plain string, so it matches only an operation whose value *is* the string `\"*\"`. Since no real operation has a scope named `\"*\"`, `scope: [\"*\"]` matches **nothing** and the processor silently receives zero operations.\n\nThe correct way to match all scopes is to omit the field or set it to `[]`:\n\n```typescript\nconst filter: ProcessorFilter = {\n  documentType: [\"powerhouse/todo\"],\n  scope: [\"global\"],   // concrete scopes, or omit entirely\n  branch: [\"main\"],\n  documentId: [\"*\"],   // \"*\" is honored here\n};\n```\n\nThis is not hypothetical: the docs flag a **latent inconsistency** in the codegen analytics template, which emits `scope: [\"*\"]`. Scaffold an analytics processor and you get a filter that cannot match, with no error to tell you. Set `scope` to the concrete scopes you want, or drop the key.\n\nBe aware this **contradicts** the guidance in the existing note \"Processors implement IProcessor with onOperations\u2026\", which states that `ProcessorFilter` arrays take `[\"*\"]` for all. That generalization holds for `documentId` and fails for the other three fields; the per-field rule above is the current behavior.\n\nAlso note the division of labour: the filter decides *which* operations reach a processor, while `startFrom` on the `ProcessorRecord` decides *where in history* it starts. They are unrelated knobs.\n\n_Source: apps/academy/docs/academy/04-Reference/01-Reactor/05-Processors.md (Powerhouse v6.2.2-dev.45)._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-13T14:34:29.000Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-13T14:34:30.231Z"
    }
  },
